      "problem": "Given that $x=2$ is a root of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-14x+8,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
      "worked_solution": "First, we notice that the function is a negative cubic curve because the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is negative.\n\nWe are told that $x=2$ is a root of $f(x),$ which means that $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ So, to find the other roots of $f(x)$ we use synthetic division to divide $f(x)$ by $(x-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 2 & -1 & 7 & -14 & 8 \\\\  &  & -2 & 10 & -8 \\\\  & -1 & 5 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-2)(-{x}^{2}+5x-4) \\\\  & =-(x-2)({x}^{2}-5x+4) \\\\  & =-(x-2)(x-1)(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to zero, we find that the other roots of $f(x)$ are $x=1$ and $x=4.$ \n\nNow, let's find the $y$-intercept. To do that, we substitute $x=0,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(0-2)(0-1)(0-4) \\\\  & =-(-2)(-1)(-4) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an $S$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=1,2,4$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=8,$ as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250730/assets/21dec37151f40778b8f094d3ae68cd8716c993535e53496cf7f148817c72e345.png)",
